## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Adam Finney is a CHERI technologist with over 20 years of systems experience, and a key driver of CHERI adoption. As Lead of the Adoption Programme and Marketing Working Group, he focuses on turning security into something developers and businesses can actually use. Adam has worked at the sharp end of the stack—from porting Linux to Morello to shaping next‑generation CHERI‑enabled FPGA platforms and developer boards.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Alex Rebert is an engineer at Google, where he focuses on memory safety. Previously, he co-founded ForAllSecure and led the creation of Mayhem, the autonomous cyber reasoning system that won the 2016 DARPA Cyber Grand Challenge. He was recognized by MIT Technology Review as one of the 35 Innovators Under 35 and by Forbes’s 30 Under 30.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Alex Richardson represents Google on the CHERI Alliance Governing Board. His board role is Senior Software Engineer.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Allison Randal represents Capabilities Limited on the CHERI Alliance Governing Board. Her board role is Open Source/Hardware Strategist.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Andrew Lindsay represents Codasip on the CHERI Alliance Governing Board. His board role is Security Product Manager and Architect.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Andy Moore drives awareness and adoption of RISC‑V across its global member ecosystem. As Director of Marketing at RISC‑V International, and with deep prior experience at Arm, he brings a rare mix of processor IP marketing and hands‑on engineering.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Ben Fletcher has been involved in SoC development for over 25 years. He has worked in design, verification, software, and more recently Architecture. He has worked for Codasip for 2 years, and is currently working on performance models and architectures of out-of-order cores, along with SoC developments (all featuring CHERI technology).

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Bradley Finn is Head of Cyber Innovation Policy at the UK’s Department for Science, Innovation and Technology. He is responsible for polices and programmes to support the growth of the cyber sector across the UK and help innovators develop world leading cyber tech.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Dave Harcourt is the CISO for BT International and a BT Fellow, with decades of experience shaping cyber security at scale. He sets security strategy for BT and influences direction across industry, driven by a clear principle: make secure‑by‑default the easiest path to follow. His focus is on security that actually gets adopted.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

David Chisnall has been working on CHERI since 2012, when he joined the University of Cambridge to lead the languages and compiler work on the CHERI project. He joined Microsoft in 2018, where he adapted several in-house ISAs to CHERI, worked on high-performance server-class CHERI implementations, and also created and led the CHERIoT project, which Microsoft open sourced in 2023. He is a co-founder of SCI Semiconductor, which is building CHERIoT microcontrollers. Along with Yucong Tao (Microsoft) and Ben Laurie (Google), he is a co-maintainer of the CHERIoT platform.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Dr. David Musliner is a Senior Research Fellow at Smart Information Flow Technologies (SIFT).

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Dmitriy is an embedded systems software architect and technical expert with over 25 years of experience. Their career began in telecommunications, developing embedded software for network systems. Over time, they transitioned into industrial automation and later specialized in safety-critical industries such as medical devices, aerospace, and defense. Since 2010, They have been with Wind River GmbH, focusing on system architecture, advanced technology research, and compliance with industry standards. Previously, they contributed to software architecture and embedded solutions at Hermes SoftLab and worked on telecommunication infrastructure at IskraTEL. They hold a Master’s degree in Computing and Intelligent Systems &amp; Networks from the National University of Radio Electronics in Kharkiv, Ukraine.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Domagoj Stolfa is a researcher currently working at Capabilities Limited and University of Cambridge. He’s done work on kernels, hypervisors, tracing systems and language runtimes. He implemented initial libunwind support for CheriBSD’s c18n and has since been working on V8 and its integration into the Chromium web browser.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Edoardo has been working with compilers for the majority of his professional career. After working in the Wasm space, he joined SCI Semiconductor and started working on this project since its beginning. He has been involved with every aspect of it, ranging from technical tasks such as adding the CHERIoT target to organising work for the team and communicating about the project to make it more visible.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Details to follow.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Javier is a business leader with over 25 years of experience in the semiconductors industry. He was the general manager of Arm’s tools business unit for 8 years, and is currently CEO of lowRISC. He holds a degree in telecommunication engineering and an MBA.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Jennifer is an Electronic Engineer with experience in FPGA design, Arm v8.2, computer security, capability architectures, CHERI, LLVM tool chain, RISC-V development and embedded and Zephyr OS development for CHERI.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Jeremy is a Reader in Programming Language Implementation at the University of Glasgow. He is the proud owner (or at least, custodian) of four Arm Morello boxes and two lowRISC Sonata boards.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

John Baldwin has been an active FreeBSD developer for over 25 years working on a broad range of subsystems in the kernel and bits of userspace. John has also contributed to GDB. John began working with the CHERI project for over 10 years including work in CheriBSD and CHERI GDB and more recently dabbling in CHERI LLVM and Morello LLVM.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Kathleen Fisher has been instrumental in bringing CHERI from research into national programmes. A long-standing leader in secure systems and programming languages, she has championed CHERI through major DARPA initiatives. She has now joined ARIA as a CEO to continue connecting deep technical foundations with real-world impact and deployment.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Kevin Brodsky is a software engineer at Arm, specializing in the Morello project and the Morello Linux kernel.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Louis-Emile is a current undergraduate at the University of Oxford, and a formal verification engineer for lowRISC working on the COSMIC project.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Luca Müller received the M.Sc. degree in computer science from University of Bremen in 2025. He is currently a Researcher in the Cyber-Physical Systems Group at the German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence (DFKI) and is pursuing his Ph.D. at the University of Bremen. His research interest lies in the verification of hardware designs at different abstraction levels, with a focus on formal techniques. Other research interests include system security and LLMs for hardware verification.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Maika Föhrenbach connects CHERI to Europe’s regulatory future. She leads product security and certification policy at the European Commission and coordinates implementation of the Cyber Resilience Act.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Merve Gülmez is a researcher at Ericsson Security Research. She holds a PhD in Computer Science from KU Leuven, and her research focuses on systems security, memory safety, and improving software resilience.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Details to follow.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Murat is Co-CEO @ Microservice Store.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Dr Paul Metzger is a Research Associate in the Department of Computer Science and Technology at the University of Cambridge. He recently started working on CHERI OS API portability and has been involved with CHERI projects since 2021. Prior to that, he received his PhD from the University of Edinburgh.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Paul Waller works on advancing practical, deployable cyber security at national scale. At the NCSC, he focuses on how technologies like CHERI and memory safety translate into real risk reduction and adoption across critical systems.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Phil is the Director of Engineering at Configured Things, a startup founded by Alumni from Hewlett Packard Labs to build solutions that can operate resiliently across security domains. He has been a regular speaker at forums such as the IoT Security Foundation conference and CyberCon, and is a contributor to the CHERIoT ecosystem and a technical reviewer for the CHERIoT programmers’ Guide. He has more years experience than he cares to admit to developing and delivering complex distributed systems.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Samuel W. Stark is a final-year PhD student at the University of Cambridge, studying alternate capability systems under Simon W. Moore, with a published article on the topic in Communications of the ACM in collaboration with Simon W. Moore and A. Theodore Markettos.

## Biography
{: id="biography" }

Tora Fridholm is an experienced B2B marketing professional with a focus on transforming complex technology into clear, value-driven messaging. Currently, she leads marketing and communications at Codasip, a processor technology company, where she drives messaging, launches, and media communications. Previously, Tora served as Chief Marketing Officer at IAR Systems, overseeing global marketing strategies and leading cross-functional teams. With a Master of Arts in Computational Linguistics from Uppsala University, Tora combines her technical background with a passion for innovation to align marketing efforts with company strategy and build impactful partnerships.

