## Description
{: id="description" }

The Defence Science and Technology Laboratory (Dstl) delivers high-impact science and technology (S&amp;T) solutions to enhance the UK’s defence and security. As an executive agency of the UK Ministry of Defence (MOD), Dstl works closely with academia, industry and international allies to advance innovative technologies that provides operational advantage. Dstl supplies specialist services to MOD and wider government, working collaboratively with external partners in industry and academia worldwide, providing expert research, specialist advice and invaluable operational support.

## CHERI Activities
{: id="cheri-activities" }

In 2023, the MOD Reducing Cyber Attack Surface research project ran a competition (via Defence and Security Accelerator) to grow MOD’s understanding of the potential benefits or challenges of deploying CHERI processor technologies within Defence and Security systems. This competition was also intended to increase the base of potential defence suppliers with experience of Morello/CHERI, to provide a pool of future innovation the UK can draw on. Twenty nine proposals were received of which sixteen were chosen from a broad cross-section of suppliers and applications. These projects concluded in March 2024. In 2022, as part of the RAF Rapid Capability Office ongoing program, Dstl started the research and development of the Edge Avionics with Digital Security by Design project. This joint R&amp;D project involves an industry consortia of GE Aviation UK, as leader, Windriver and Adacore. The project is creating a TSN network of remote data concentrators to demonstrate a legacy air mission system executing on Morello/CHERI systems. The initial aims is to prototype novel distributed architectures with CHERI/Morello using real avionics applications and to create a secure by design avionics system for further research. As of November 2024, the project is continuing with an expectation of the experimental system being complete in March 2025. The project has already led to the successful demonstration of Adacore tooling to support Ada program development on Morello.

## Description
{: id="description" }

The Department of Computer Science, based in the beautiful historic city of Durham, is one of the leading UK departments with an outstanding reputation for excellence in teaching, research and employability of our students. Computer Science increased excellence in all areas of research, moving 6 places up the ranking to the 20th position for REF 2021. It is ranked 10th in the Guardian University Guide 2024, and 4th for student employability in the Complete University Guide 2026.

## CHERI Activities
{: id="cheri-activities" }

Prof David Oswald’s group led CAP-TEE project (EPSRC grant EP/V000454/1), funded under the UKRI Digital Security by Design Challenge. The project used capability architectures to protect Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs) against state-of-the-art attacks, addressing threats ranging from software vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows to hardware attacks. This project resulted in a number of research outputs using and extending CHERI. Following on from that, the group now contributes to CHERI-Zephyr, a fork of the widely used Zephyr RTOS adapted to incorporate CHERI capability support. The repository has reached version 0.3.0 (released February 2026) with active ongoing development.

## Open-source Assets for CHERI
{: id="open-source-assets-for-cheri" }

[https://github.com/CHERI-Alliance/CHERI-zephyr](https://github.com/CHERI-Alliance/CHERI-zephyr)

## Description
{: id="description" }

The FreeBSD Foundation is a 501(c)(3) non-profit organization supporting the FreeBSD Project and community. Accepting donations from individuals and businesses, the Foundation uses funds to develop features, employ software engineers, improve build and test infrastructure, advocate for FreeBSD through in-person and online events, and provide training and educational material. Representing the FreeBSD Project in legal affairs, the Foundation is the recognized entity for contracts, licenses, and other legal arrangements and is entirely donation supported.

## CHERI Activities
{: id="cheri-activities" }

CheriBSD extends FreeBSD to implement memory protection and software compartmentalization features supported by the CHERI instruction-set extensions. There are two active architectural implementations of the CHERI protection model: CHERI-RISC-V and Arm’s Morello. A sketch of CHERI-x86 is also under development. CheriBSD is a research operating system with a stable baseline implementation into which various new research features have been, or are currently being, merged.

## Open-source Assets for CHERI
{: id="open-source-assets-for-cheri" }

[https://www.cheribsd.org/](https://www.cheribsd.org/)

## Description
{: id="description" }

Google’s mission is to organize the world’s information and make it universally accessible and useful.

## CHERI Activities
{: id="cheri-activities" }

Google has collaborated with the University of Cambridge on the CHERI project, providing funding and engineering expertise to support its development and implementation. Google is engaged in security research related to CHERI, exploring its potential to enhance software security and mitigate vulnerabilities. This research helps to identify and address potential challenges and refine the CHERI architecture.

## Description
{: id="description" }

The School of Engineering of the Polytechnic Institute of Porto (ISEP), Portugal, is a public higher education and research institution, and one of the largest schools of engineering in Portugal (6000 students), with a strong diversity of subject areas. The Software Technologies for Cyber-Physical Systems (SoftCPS) Laboratory of ISEP team has been focusing on R&amp;D activity on embedded and real-time computing systems, comprising more than 20 years of experience on R&amp;D in the design of safe and secure embedded systems.

## CHERI Activities
{: id="cheri-activities" }

SoftCPS@ISEP is developing safe and secure embedded systems by advancing state-of-the-art software technologies that are tightly aligned with modern hardware safety features. The lab explores high-integrity programming languages such as Ada and Rust to enforce memory safety, or real-time operating systems like Zephyr, enabling predictable, reliable execution in resource-constrained embedded environments, exploring emerging safety-oriented architectures such as CHERI, to enforce fine-grained spatial and temporal memory protection.

## Description
{: id="description" }

Johannes Kepler University (JKU) Linz is a public research university in Linz, Upper Austria. Located on a modern campus just outside the city center, JKU brings together a broad range of study and research across its faculties and interdisciplinary institutes. With a strong focus on innovation, international collaboration, and close ties to industry, JKU offers an excellent environment for studying, teaching, and research—preparing graduates to shape the future in business, science, and society.

## CHERI Activities
{: id="cheri-activities" }

The Institute for Complex Systems (ICS) at JKU Linz focuses on mastering the ever-increasing complexity of modern hardware/software systems. Our work spans multiple abstraction levels—from Virtual Prototypes (VPs) in SystemC at the Electronic System Level (ESL), to RTL designs in Verilog/VHDL, down to gate-level representations. Across these levels, we tackle core challenges in Electronic Design Automation (EDA), with a strong emphasis on verification, debugging, and synthesis. A key pillar of this effort is our open-source virtual prototyping ecosystem around RISC-V. As a concrete outcome, we provide RISC-V VP++, a highly-configurable virtual prototype used for system-level evaluation, design exploration and novel verification approaches. Since 2025, RISC-V VP++ has CHERI support.

## Open-source Assets for CHERI
{: id="open-source-assets-for-cheri" }

[https://github.com/ics-jku/riscv-vp-plusplus](https://github.com/ics-jku/riscv-vp-plusplus)

